INT. BOXING RING - CORNER - NIGHT

A hot arena. A roaring crowd that is only noise. Bright ropes, spit buckets, a canvas stained the colour of old tea.

PRISCILLA "PRISS" VANTONGEREN, twenties, in red trunks, sits on the stool between rounds, chest heaving, a swollen eye closing. She wears a wireless earpiece. Her coach, old MEL BRANDVOLD, sixty-eight, stands at ringside two states away on a hospital bed phone, his voice crackling through the bud.

MEL (V.O.)
(over earpiece)
Priss. Priss, look at me. Can you see?

PRISS
I can see.

MEL (V.O.)
How many fingers?

PRISS
I can't see you, Mel. You're in Tulsa.

MEL (V.O.)
Humour me.

PRISS
Three.

MEL (V.O.)
Good girl. Listen. Round seven. She drops her left every time she throws the hook.

PRISS
I know.

MEL (V.O.)
Step in and counter.

PRISS
What if I'm too tired?

MEL (V.O.)
Priss, I've seen you tired. I've never seen you quit.

The bell rings. She rises, mouthguard in, gloves up. Across the ring her opponent circles, taller, quicker. Priss bobs, weaves, takes a jab on the shoulder, slips a second, and steps in. The hook sails past her ear.

PRISS
(muttering)
Now.

She throws the right. It lands. The opponent staggers, drops her guard, and Priss follows with the left, a short chopping blow that snaps the head back and sends the crowd to its feet.

MEL (V.O.)
(over earpiece, shouting)
That's it! That's my girl!

The referee steps between. Priss stands over her opponent, trembling.
